# Python practices and reuse

## Best practices

- Prefer small, single-purpose functions and explicit names; keep modules focused.
- Match existing patterns in the repo (imports, typing style, error handling, logging).
- Use typed signatures and structured data (`TypedDict`, dataclasses, or Pydantic) consistently with the rest of the project.
- Put configuration in settings/env; avoid hardcoding secrets or magic strings that already exist as constants elsewhere.

## Do not duplicate—reuse first

- Before adding logic, search the codebase for similar behavior (same parsing, HTTP calls, path handling, CLI patterns, etc.).
- Prefer extending an existing function or module over copying blocks; extract shared helpers when two call sites need the same steps.
- If duplication is only avoidable with heavy abstraction, prefer one clear shared helper over two slightly divergent copies—then unify call sites.

## When touching tests

- Reuse fixtures and helpers under `tests/`; do not copy large setup blocks across files without extracting a shared fixture or utility.
